@node Troubleshooting
@chapter Troubleshooting
@cindex troubleshooting

This chapter covers the most common failures you are likely to encounter,
their root causes, and how to fix them.

@menu
* No Plan or an Empty Reply::       The planner could not produce a plan.
* Model Timeout::                   Inference takes too long and the call fails.
* HTTP 500 from Ollama::            The Ollama runner crashes mid-inference.
* Ollama Unreachable Through a Proxy::  A proxy answers instead of a remote Ollama.
* Invalid JSON from Planner::       The model wraps output in code fences.
* Safety Rejection::                A plan step is blocked by the safety engine.
* Embedding Dimension Mismatch::    Old stub vectors mix with new real vectors.
* Web Search Returns No Results::   The DuckDuckGo scraper finds nothing.
* Tests Cannot Import codebud::     The package is not on the path.
@end menu


@node No Plan or an Empty Reply
@section No Plan, or an Empty Reply
@cindex plan_error
@cindex chat reply instead of a plan

When the planner cannot produce a valid plan --- no model answered, the
answer was not valid JSON even after one retry, or a step broke the safety
policy --- Codebud does not print a planner error.  It asks the model for a
plain conversational answer instead and prints that; if no model is
reachable, the answer is empty.  So a missing plan, an empty reply, or a
chatty answer to a request that should have produced a plan all point to
the causes below.  Run @command{codebud doctor} first: it checks most of
them.  (A plan refused because of the agent mode is reported directly; see
@ref{Agent Modes}.)

@table @strong
@item Cause: no LLM backend configured
Neither @env{OLLAMA_MODEL} nor @env{OPENAI_API_KEY} is set.

@emph{Fix:} set @env{OLLAMA_MODEL}:
@example
export OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M
@end example

@item Cause: Ollama server is not running
@env{OLLAMA_MODEL} is set but @code{curl http://localhost:11434/api/tags}
returns @samp{Connection refused}.

@emph{Fix:} start the server:
@example
ollama serve
# or: sudo systemctl start ollama
@end example

@item Cause: model not pulled
@env{OLLAMA_MODEL} names a model that is not installed.

@emph{Fix:} pull it:
@example
ollama pull qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M
@end example

@item Cause: request timed out
The model is too slow for the hardware.  See @ref{Model Timeout}.

@item Cause: a step broke the safety policy
See @ref{Safety Rejection}.
@end table


@node Model Timeout
@section Model Timeout
@cindex timeout, model inference
@cindex CPU-only inference speed

On CPU-only hardware, inference is much slower than on a machine with a GPU.
Measured on an Intel i7-8550U (2018 laptop CPU) with the 3B model and Ollama
0.40.6 (2026-10-24):

@multitable @columnfractions .40 .20 .40
@headitem Phase @tab Time @tab Notes
@item Prefill, first request (~1,046 tokens) @tab ~30 s @tab Processing the whole planning prompt
@item Prefill, later requests @tab ~2--2.6 s @tab Ollama reuses the cached start of the prompt
@item Whole request, prompt cached @tab ~8 s @tab Typical @command{codebud plan}
@end multitable

@noindent
The first call after starting the server is slower still because the model
loads from disk into RAM.  A request gives up after 600 seconds in total.

@strong{Do not use @code{stream=False}} when calling the Ollama API on slow
hardware.  With streaming disabled, the HTTP connection sits silent for the
entire prefill phase before the first byte arrives, causing a read timeout.
Codebud uses @code{stream=True} internally to avoid this.

@strong{If timeouts persist:}

@itemize
@item Switch to a smaller model (@pxref{Choosing a Model}): the 1.5B model
is 3--4x faster than the 3B on the same CPU.
@item Check that no other process is competing for RAM --- another model
loaded by Ollama will slow inference significantly.
@item On Arch Linux, set the CPU governor to @samp{performance} mode:
@example
sudo cpupower frequency-set -g performance
@end example
@item Long conversations make the prompt longer: use @code{/compact} in
@command{chat} to summarise older turns (@pxref{The chat Subcommand}).
@end itemize


@node HTTP 500 from Ollama
@section HTTP 500 from Ollama
@cindex HTTP 500
@cindex Ollama runner crash

If Ollama returns HTTP 500 and the server log (@file{/tmp/ollama.log} or
@command{journalctl -u ollama}) shows the runner process exiting, the most
likely cause is the Linux OOM (out-of-memory) killer terminating the runner
mid-inference.

@strong{Diagnosis:}

@example
dmesg | grep -i "killed process\|oom"
free -h
@end example

@strong{Fixes:}

@itemize
@item Close other large applications to free RAM.
@item Switch to a smaller model that fits more comfortably in available RAM.
@item Reduce the KV-cache size by asking for a smaller context window, for
example @code{export CODEBUD_NUM_CTX=4096} (the default is 8192); longer
conversations are then trimmed sooner.
@item Do not load two models simultaneously --- Ollama keeps a model in RAM
until its keep-alive timeout expires (default 5 minutes).  Run
@command{ollama ps} to see what is loaded.
@end itemize


@node Ollama Unreachable Through a Proxy
@section Ollama Unreachable Through a Proxy
@cindex proxy, Ollama unreachable

@strong{Symptom:} @command{codebud doctor} reports that it cannot reach
Ollama, or @command{codebud models} prints an error such as
@samp{503 Server Error: Forwarding failure}, although @command{ollama
list} works.

@strong{Cause:} an HTTP proxy is configured (@env{HTTP_PROXY}, for
example a local Privoxy) and requests to a @emph{remote} Ollama are being
sent to the proxy, which cannot forward them.  Requests to a local Ollama
are never sent through a proxy (@pxref{Configuration}).

@strong{Fix:} add the Ollama host to @env{NO_PROXY}:

@example
export NO_PROXY=192.168.1.50
codebud doctor
@end example

Versions of Codebud before this fix sent even @code{localhost} requests
through the proxy; with those, set @code{NO_PROXY=localhost,127.0.0.1}.


@node Invalid JSON from Planner
@section Invalid JSON from Planner
@cindex JSON parsing, code fences
@cindex code fences, markdown

Some models wrap their JSON output in markdown code fences:

@example
```json
@{"status": "ok", "plan": [...]@}
```
@end example

Codebud's planner strips these fences automatically before parsing
(@code{LLMPlanner._strip_code_fence} in @file{src/codebud/planner.py}).  If
the output is still not valid JSON, the planner retries once with a
shorter, stricter prompt; if that fails too, Codebud answers with a plain
chat reply instead of a plan (@pxref{No Plan or an Empty Reply}).

If the model returns something other than JSON entirely (reasoning text,
an apology, etc.), the prompt needs tuning.  The planner prompt instructs
the model to return JSON only; some models ignore this for complex
requests.  Simplify the request or switch to a larger model.


@node Safety Rejection
@section Safety Rejection
@cindex safety engine, rejection
@cindex plan step rejected

A plan whose step breaks the safety policy is rejected as a whole.
Today this is not reported as such: Codebud falls back to a plain chat
reply (@pxref{No Plan or an Empty Reply}).  The @code{SafetyEngine} rejects
a @code{command} step when its words do not begin with an entry in
@file{harmless_commands.json} or @file{powerful_commands.json} (matched by
whole words), or when it uses an argument that is always refused, such as
@code{find -exec} or @code{python -c} (@pxref{The Safety Engine}).  A step
refused because of the agent mode is reported directly instead.

@strong{To diagnose:}

Use the @command{plan} subcommand to inspect what the planner produced
without executing anything:

@example
codebud plan --json "your request here"
@end example

This prints the raw JSON result.  To see the planner's own verdict
(including @code{plan_error} and the steps it proposed), call the Python
API directly:

@example
python -c "
from codebud.core import AgentCore
import json
a = AgentCore()
plan = a.planner.generate_plan('your request here')
print(json.dumps(plan, indent=2))
"
@end example

If the plan contains a @code{command} step with an unexpected @code{cmd},
either:

@itemize
@item Add the command to your policy if it is genuinely safe: to
@file{harmless_commands.json} if it only reads, or to
@file{powerful_commands.json} if it runs code or changes files (those are
confirmed before running).  Put your copy in @file{~/.config/codebud/} or
the project's @file{.codebud/}; @command{codebud config} shows which files
are in effect (@pxref{Policy JSON Files}).
@item Rephrase the request so the planner uses a different tool (e.g.
@code{file_read} instead of @code{cat}).
@end itemize


@node Embedding Dimension Mismatch
@section Embedding Dimension Mismatch
@cindex embedding dimension mismatch

If you used Codebud before the real Ollama embedding backend was wired up,
@file{$XDG_DATA_HOME/codebud/embeddings/index.json} (by default under
@file{~/.local/share}) may contain 16-dimensional stub vectors.
New embeddings are 2048-dimensional.  Cosine similarity between entries of
different sizes returns 0.0, making search results incorrect.

@strong{Fix:} clear the index and re-embed your content:

@example
echo "[]" > "$@{XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share@}/codebud/embeddings/index.json"
@end example

Then re-run whatever pipeline populated the index.


@node Web Search Returns No Results
@section Web Search Returns No Results
@cindex web search, no results
@cindex DuckDuckGo

If @code{WEB_SEARCH_ENABLED=1} is set but the tool returns an empty list,
DuckDuckGo Lite's HTML structure may have changed, breaking the regex
scraper.

@strong{To diagnose:}

@example
python -c "
import requests
r = requests.post('https://lite.duckduckgo.com/lite/',
    data=@{'q': 'test'@},
    headers=@{'User-Agent': 'Mozilla/5.0'@}, timeout=10)
print(r.text[:2000])
"
@end example

Look for @samp{result-link} and @samp{result-snippet} class names.  If
they have changed, update the regex patterns in
@file{src/codebud/tools/web_search.py:_ddg_search}.


@node Tests Cannot Import codebud
@section Tests Cannot Import @code{codebud}
@cindex pytest, ImportError
@cindex editable install

If @command{pytest} reports:

@example
ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'codebud'
@end example

it is running with a Python that cannot see the package.  Either run the
tests through the build, which puts @file{src/} on the path:

@example
make check
@end example

@noindent
or set up the development environment and use its Python
(@pxref{Build System}):

@example
./bootstrap --dev
.venv/bin/python -m pytest tests
@end example
